lens: the thing at cur, seen its ways, as the tabs of one slot

A lens is a way to see the thing at cur, made from it and never typed in
(`design/studio:/a lens shows a thing/`). The lens view is a slot of the
frame whose tabs are the thing's source and its lenses, one shown at a
time, Tab turning to the next:

	source     the text as written, the one tab typed in: ed -f
	outline    its parts, as rows that fold, or as thumbs with t, as fm
	           lays out a folder: studio show outline
	render     what the text makes for a person to see: its parts set
	           for the eye, or a note's picture when it is written as
	           parts and messages: studio show render
	diff       what changed since the last commit, as git says it:
	           studio show diff

The reader, by kind
  The outline and the render are one tree, which the reader for the
  thing's kind makes of it, the right tool, picked by a line as sh/o
  picks an opener (`studio/4-lens/26-readers.go:/var readers/`):
	a note     its parts as its writer marks them (outline.Parts)
	Go         its declarations, by go/parser (outline.Go)
	Python     its classes and defs, by page/py -t, a program, as pic
	           is troff's
	the rest   its lines, by their indent, the floor (outline.Text)
  The outline draws the tree's parts, a row each, and folds them, the way
  to the cursor unfolded; the render draws all of it set as nroff sets a
  page, prose filled to a measure, a table's names in a column, a name
  bold, an address underlined. The lens says what each part is, and how
  is the view's (`design/view:/Kinds, never looks/`). A reader joins as a
  line, and a way to see a thing joins the lenses as one
  (`studio/4-lens/02-lenses.go:/var lenses/`) and the lens view as a tab.

The idea: a frame is slots, and a slot is tabs
	frame      slots in the order they take room, laid out as a golden
	           spiral, over the panel (layout)
	slot       a place in the frame, by name; its tabs take turns there
	tab        one program, by name; the slot's tab line names its tabs
	panel      the slot shell, across the frame's bottom: its tabs are
	           terminals, each a shell or a command
	studio     owns all four, in tmux and in gio's window alike; no
	           program knows of them
The lens view is a slot of tabs, and so is the panel: l new opens a
terminal as a tab of it, and what spawn opens in a frame comes there
too. More terminals are tabs, not splits, and the panel's tab line
numbers them, 1 2 3. Their keys are in `studio/cheatsheet`.

The case, carried through: design/arch at fm's cursor
	the frame   fm at the left and the lens view at the right, 62% of the
	            width, over the panel across the bottom, 38% of the
	            height; alt-t hides the panel and shows it
	source      ed edits design/arch; its tab line, source  outline
	            render  diff, is the border over the slot, source lit
	Tab         in ed runs l, and the outline shows in the slot's place,
	            from the text as ed holds it, unsaved lines too
	j, enter    the next part, said to cur, so ed's cursor goes to its
	            line; l unfolds a part, h folds it, and enter opens it,
	            the source showing there
	Tab Tab     the render, then the diff; Tab again, the source

Layout: the golden spiral, over the panel
  The panel, the first slot that keeps a shell, goes across the frame's
  bottom, 38% of its height, hidden and shown by studio panel, its room
  going to the slots above. The rest take the room above
  it: the first 1/φ of it, 62%, at its right;
  each after it 62% of what is left, down and across in turn; the last
  what remains, so the rest go on toward the bottom left and the first
  never shrinks for them (`studio/1-frame/19-layout.go`). It is one tree
  for every host: tmux carves it, gio lays it out from studio slots, and
  the page draws it. No share is written: a slot's place in the order is
  its room.

What ed says, and how the lens follows
  ed is an editor: it holds the text and changes it, and says nothing of
  its structure. In a frame it does two things more, which any program
  can use: it puts what it holds unsaved in the window's text file, once a
  burst of keys is done (`ed/7-loop/14-share.go`), and its Tab runs l, as
  a right click runs p (`ed/6-commands/68-tab.go`). Each lens tab follows
  cur, as fm -f and ed -f do, and reads the text file in place of the
  note while the window's unsaved file names it
  (`studio/4-lens/13-noted.go`). A key on a row says the row to cur, and
  ed -f, whose file it is, puts its cursor there, unsaved or not
  (`ed/5-editor/15-chase.go:/Within its own file/`).

The tabs in tmux
  A slot's pane shows its first tab; the rest wait in a window each of
  the frame's back session, out of the window list, and a tab shown trades
  places with the one there (`studio/2-term/14-show.go`). Each tab's pane
  carries its slot, @slot, its name, @tab, and its frame, @frame; e asks
  studio to show the source tab, by its name (`sh/e:/studio tab source/`).
  The tab line is the slot's, drawn on the border over it in the
  palette's chips (`studio/2-term/12-bar/01-bar.go`), and each name on it
  is a button: tmux marks ranges on a border line, each with a mouse key
  of its own, and a press on one sends studio tab what l would, the tab
  at that place, or the panel's new terminal or close
  (`tmux/conf:/a frame's tab line/`). A tab that ends gives its place to
  the next (`studio/2-term/22-died.go`), and the frame's end ends the
  tabs waiting with it, but a terminal running a command, which comes
  back to the window (`studio/5-verbs/10-closeverb.go`).

The tabs in gio's window
  The window runs each tab and shows one in its slot's place, the slot's
  tab line over it, each tab's name a button
  (`gio/look.go:/func (s *studio) tabBar/`), and takes the words tmux's
  frame takes, as sys/tabs says them (`tabs/tabs.go:/func Turn/`). It has no
  tmux to ask, so studio tab and studio panel add what they ask to the
  window's ask file, a line an ask, which the window does in order as it
  hears it (`studio/5-verbs/22-ask/02-ask.go`); ed and the lens tell l
  the slot they run in, which the window has no pane to say, and what
  the window starts is told its frame, $STUDIO_WINDOW, so that a program
  in no frame asks nothing of it. Whether the panel is hidden the window
  alone keeps. The panel's terminals are the window's own, each a shell
  or a command on a pty, its screen st's as sys/vt keeps it
  (`gio/term.go`), and its tab line has + and ×, as tmux's has. A shell
  is told no slot, so that what runs from it is in no frame, and l typed
  there asks of the slot with the eye, the one it was typed in.

Read, never typed
  No key types in the outline, the render or the diff, so none changes the
  text out of sight. A move says where it is to cur; enter, or a right
  click, opens what the row names, and in the render a right click opens
  the word it falls on, as in ed
  (`studio/4-lens/08-run.go:/func (l *lensing) run/`). Run alone, outside a
  frame, studio show has every lens, Tab turning them, its state saying
  the one shown.

What is where
	ed                    the source: the text, and what it holds
	                      unsaved, said
	studio show lens      one lens of the lens view (`studio/4-lens`)
	studio tab, l         a slot's tabs turned, shown, opened, closed
	                      (`studio/5-verbs/21-turn.go`)
	sys/tabs              what each of those words means, once, for
	                      tmux and gio's window alike
	studio panel          the terminals hidden and shown
	studio/cheatsheet     the keys, the tab line and l, for use
	the frame's layout    the golden spiral over the panel
	                      (`studio/1-frame`)
	sys/view              the outline as rows that fold, the render set
	                      as nroff sets a page, and the diff as rows
	gio's window          the same tabs, its tab line's names buttons
	sys/vt                a terminal, st's: the window's panel's
	                      terminals
	outline.Note          the note an address names, for ed and the lens
	the readers           a note's parts, Go's declarations, Python's
	                      by page/py, the rest by its indent
	                      (`studio/4-lens/26-readers.go`)
	sys/picture           a note's picture, its render when it is
	                      written as parts and messages

What it costs
  - a program a lens, each following cur, and a file between them and ed:
    what ed holds unsaved, put whole at each pause in typing
  - a reader's run each time the text changes, and only then: a move
    marks the cursor on the tree the lens made and draws once, and the
    view sets a render once for a tree and a width; Python's reader is a
    program, a third of a second for a file of a thousand lines
  - a line over the frame for the tab lines, once a slot has one
  - outside a frame ed keeps no text file, and has no tabs: Tab there says
    so

Not built
  - a tab of splits, a terminal split in two (`todo/03-studio/10-panes`)
  - the tabs on the page

Given up
  - the lens in ed, Tab turning ed's pane: each way to see the text made
    ed bigger, and ed is an editor
  - the lens in a slot of its own under ed: two panes showed one note
  - shares written in the frame, and a lens file turned by its own verb:
    the order gives the room, and the slot's tabs are the lenses
  - a menu of a slot's tabs on a press on its line: two presses to show
    one, once tmux could mark each name a button
